      {
        "review_id": 556028362,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r556028362-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "One of the holiest Hindu Temple of Lord Shiva ",
        "text": "This Head of the12 Jyotirlinga shrines of Lord Shiva is the major attraction of Kathmandu. The original construction of the temple is unknown. The current structure was built in 15th Century by Lichhabi Kingdom. The temple complex is located 5 km from city centre closed to the airport on the river Bagmati. Around  the main 2 storied temple complex you will find Rama temple, Guhyeswari temple and Vaishnabi temple. The temple wooden door is beautiful and a must see. Once you enter the gate the first thing you see is the large golden statue of Nandi, the famous bull of Lord Shiva. The tempke area is about 264 hectares with total 518 temples inside. You need to keep your shoes in one of the shops from you can buy offerings at NPR 150 onwards. They will provide you rubber sleepers which you need to keep near the main gate and enter bare foot. We visited pn a Wednesday and fortunately there was very less crowd inside. We could go close to the deity and quietly give our offerings peacefully. We did a complete parikrama of the temple. The main burning ghat of Kathmandu lies behind the temple complex and Hindus feels it holy to have the last rites at this place. You can see the pyers burning from the backside of the temple complex. A scene not recommended if you have timid heart. LAST BUT NOT THE LEAST. DO TAKE NOTE OF ONE IMPORTANT FACT. DO NEVER TRY TO TAKE PHOTOGRAPHS ONCE YOU CROSS THE MAIN GATE AND DON'T EVEN TAKE OUT YOUR MOBILE ALSO. IT CAN GET PERMANENTLY CONFISCATED.\nThere is no entry fee in the complex.",
